<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp3sec1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>Linear Maps</title>
        <p>
            In your first course in linear algebra, you studies matrix transformations <m>T:\R^n\to \R^m</m>, <m>\vect{x}\mapsto A\vect{x}</m>, where <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and <m>A\vect{x}</m> is matrix multiplication.
            These transformations had significant algebraic and geometric interpretations and applications.
            As with all things in this course, we revisit this object and perform structural abstraction - yielding a linear map.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-linearmap">
            <title>Linear Map</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> are vector spaces over the field <m>F</m>, then a <term>linear map</term> (a.k.a. <term>linear transformation</term>) from <m>V</m> to <m>W</m> is a set map <m>T:V\to W</m> satisfying:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(L1)">
                    <li>
                        <p>(Preserves addition) <m>T(\vect{v}_1+\vect{v}_2) = T(\vect{v}_1) + T(\vect{v}_2)</m> for all <m>\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_2\in V</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>(Preserves scaling) <m>T(k\vect{v}) = k T(\vect{v})</m> for all <m>k\in F</m> and <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <proposition xml:id="prop_identity_is_linear">
            <title>The identity map is linear</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The identity map <m>\id_V:V\to V</m> is a linear map.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. You may find <xref ref="Def-do_nothing__set_map"/> and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-comp_of_linear_is_linear">
            <title>The composition of linear maps is linear</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m>, <m>W</m>, and <m>U</m> are vector spaces and <m>T_1:V_1\to V_2</m> and <m>T_2:V_2\to V_3</m> are linear maps, then the composition <m>(T_2\circ T_1): V_1\to V_3</m> is a linear map.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. You may find <xref ref="def-composition"/> and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop_lin_map_zero">
            <title>Linear maps send zero to zero</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map, then <m>T(\vect{0}_V) = \vect{0}_W</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Analyze the expression <m>T(\vect{0}_V+\vect{0}_V)</m>. You may find <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/> and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <definition xml:id="def-mixturemap">
            <title>Mixture Map</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m> and <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V</m> is a finite ordered collection of vectors, then the set map
                </p>
                <me>\Mix_S: F^n \to V, \ \ \ \begin{bmatrix} x_1\\ \vdots \\x_n\end{bmatrix} \mapsto x_1\vect{v}_1+ \cdots + x_n\vect{v}_n</me>
                <p>
                    is the <term>mixture map</term> associated to <m>S</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <p>
            You input a list of scalars and the mixture map outputs the associated linear combination. The term <q>mixture map</q> is nonstandard, but encapsulates a central concept in linear maps.
        </p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-mix_map_is_linear">
            <title>The mixture map is linear</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m> and <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V</m>, then the mixture map associated to <m>S</m>, <m>\Mix_S:F^n\to V</m>, is a linear map.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. You may find <xref ref="def-mixturemap"/>, <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/>, and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <example xml:id="ex-linearmaps-calculus">
            <title>Examples of Linear Maps from Calculus</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Many familiar operations are in fact linear maps. For example, in calculus, you wind up verifying that both differentiation and integration are linear maps in the appropriate contexts. In the symbolism of <xref ref="ex-subspaces-calculus"/>,
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>If <m>I\subset \R</m> is an interval, then the differentiation map <m>\Diff:\mathcal{D}(I,\R)\to \mathcal{C}(I,\R)</m>, <m>\Diff(f):=f'</m>, is linear.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>If <m>I=[a,b]\subset \R</m> is an interval, then the integration map <m>\In:\mathcal{C}(I,\R)\to\R</m>, <m>\In(f):=\int_a^bf(x)dx</m> is linear.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    As those verifications are outside the scope of this course, you may take the linearity of those as given without proof.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>


</section>